## 2. Two audiences, two routes

The same form is served to a signed-in member and to a visitor with no account.

| Audience | Route | Layout group |
|---|---|---|
| Member | `/programs/$programType/register` | `_appLayout` (the normal portal layout) |
| Guest | `/registration/$programType` | `_publicFormLayout` |
| Affiliate (guest only) | `/registration/affiliate` | `_publicFormLayout` |

`/registration/<type>` is the legacy public address and is already in
circulation in GARP marketing email — keep it.

**Membership sign-up lives at `/registration/affiliate` — a *static* sibling
of `/registration/$programType`, not a case inside it.** TanStack Router sorts
routes by specificity, so a static segment always matches ahead of a dynamic
one and the affiliate path never reaches the exam dispatcher. It must not:
`program-registration-panel` branches on programme slug to build an *exam*
cart, and `redirectMemberToPortalForm` would bounce a member to
`/programs/affiliate/register`, which is not a real page.

Affiliate is the only programme with **no member twin**. Sign-up creates the
GARP account, and anyone with a session already has one, so its guard is
`redirectMemberToDashboard` rather than a redirect to an in-portal form. It
also has no payment return to protect — its order is a single AFREE line for
zero, settled server-side — which is why that guard has no `isPaymentReturn`
branch while the exam one does.

`/affiliate` was the old address and still resolves, but only as a redirect
(`pages/affiliate/index.tsx`, `beforeLoad` throws, renders nothing). Link to
`AFFILIATE_REGISTRATION_ROUTE`, never to `/affiliate`.

### Never give a route its own layout group just to change its guard

The obvious way to send a guest somewhere other than Login is a second layout
group whose `beforeLoad` runs instead of `_appLayout`'s. **Do not.** Two sibling
pathless layouts are two different positions in the React tree, so crossing
between them unmounts and remounts the entire portal shell — navbar, sidebar,
footer, alert bar — and replays `PageEnterFade` over all of it. That is a
visible full-screen flash on every navigation in or out of the route, and it
also breaks the alert bar's "mounted once, never refetches" guarantee. This was
shipped and had to be undone; the give-away is that `/programs → /programs/scr`
is smooth while `/programs → /programs/frm/register` flashes.

Verify with node identity, not by eye:
`const h = document.querySelector('header')` before a client-side navigation,
then `h === document.querySelector('header')` after. Same node = no remount.

**Instead, let the one layout guard decide.** A parent `beforeLoad` runs before
any child's, so the exception belongs in the parent:
`_appLayout` asks `publicRegistrationFallback(location.pathname)`
(`lib/registration-paths.ts`) and redirects to the public twin when there is
one, falling back to Login otherwise. The member route then stays an ordinary
`_appLayout` child like its siblings.

The public route keeps its own leaf guard (`redirectMemberToPortalForm` in
`auth/registration-guard.ts`) — it lives under a different shell anyway, so
there is no shared chrome to preserve.

Guards must:

- **Resolve the session synchronously on a cache hit.** An `async beforeLoad`
  always returns a Promise, which flashes the pending shell on every
  navigation. Follow the shape in `_appLayout/route.tsx`: `undefined` = not yet
  fetched, `null` = guest, object = member.
- **Carry `search` through the redirect.** A marketing link is
  `?regCode=TEAM24`; losing it on the bounce silently reprices the order.
- **Keep a payment return's params.** See below.

### The payment return is sacred

The checkout success URL is built from `window.location` at submit time, so the
provider returns to **whichever route started the payment**, carrying `oid`/`on`
and nothing else. At that moment the order is already charged.

- Guards resolve the session but **suppress the redirect** when
  `isPaymentReturn(search)`.
- The outcome screen renders **before any query runs** — re-rendering the form
  behind a completed payment invites a second registration.

