## 5. UI/UX contract

The registration form is a **checkout**, not a settings page.

- **60/40 split** — form in `lg:col-span-6`, order summary rail in
  `lg:col-span-4`, inside `lg:grid-cols-10`. The rail is pinned
  (`lg:sticky lg:top-22 lg:h-fit`) and scrolls internally rather than with the
  form, so the running total stays on screen.
- **A sticky `top` must equal the element's natural offset, never exceed it.**
  `sticky` with a larger `top` pushes the element *down* immediately, at rest,
  on first paint — at `top-28` the rail sat 24px below the form column beside
  it and looked misaligned before anyone scrolled. The natural offset here is
  the sticky bar (4rem) + the grid gap (1.5rem) = `top-22`.
- **A pinned rail must be capped against its scroll parent, not the viewport.**
  `max-h-[calc(100vh-13.5rem)]` subtracts all three things above it — 5rem
  navbar, 3rem shell `py-6`, 5.5rem pin offset. It once read `100vh-9rem`,
  which made the rail 6rem taller than the slot it sits in: the bottom was
  clipped, and because the rail also had `overscroll-contain` the wheel would
  not chain to the page, so the total was genuinely unreachable. Do not put
  `overscroll-contain` on a secondary column — when it bottoms out the page
  should keep scrolling. **These two numbers are coupled: change the pin offset
  and this cap must follow.**
- **One sticky bar** carries back link (members only), title, live total and the
  submit button. Fully opaque — content scrolling under a translucent bar reads
  as a rendering fault. **No negative margins**: they make the bar wider than
  its scroll parent and clip the back arrow.
- **The form title is the page's `h1`.** On the public route it is the only
  heading the document has, and that page is linked from marketing email — it
  must not be an `h2` under nothing. It names the certification in full and in
  GARP's own wording, with the acronym tinted in the programme's brand colour —
  rendered through the shared `MegaMenuHeadingText` molecule and a
  `MegaMenuHeading`-shaped constant, so it matches the nav exactly and no hex is
  written by hand. Same title for both audiences.
- **Reserve space for values that arrive late.** The total block is always
  rendered at a fixed `h-10` showing `Total —` before pricing, because a block
  that appears later resizes the bar and makes the header jump.
- **Animate value changes** with `@react-spring/web` (`AnimatedAmount`), and
  blur the total while re-pricing. Never CSS transitions — see
  `animation-and-state.md`.
- **Debounce pricing** (400ms) and key the query by the request so an
  out-of-order response cannot overwrite a newer total. `keepPreviousData` holds
  the last figure so it updates rather than blinking away.

- **Every field the payload sends needs a control, or a deliberate default.**
  `smsPromotionalUpdates` sat in `buildRegisterRequest` for weeks with nothing
  rendering it, so every registration posted `false` — a marketing consent
  answered on the candidate's behalf. When adding a programme, walk the request
  builder and confirm each field is either on screen or intentionally fixed.
- **Confirm before submitting** (`ConfirmRegistrationDialog`). Order of
  operations matters: `handleSubmit` validates, the built request is then
  *staged* in state, and only the dialog's confirm fires the mutation. Past that
  point `register` writes an order and `payOrder` cannot be called twice, so the
  total gets one more look. On failure close the dialog — the error belongs
  against the form it has to be fixed in.
- **A cart control says what the click does**: `Add` ⇄ `Remove`, not `Add` ⇄
  `Added`. The row's own tint already carries "in the cart", so tinting the
  button to match reads as a confirmation rather than as the way back out.
- **Empty states keep the shape of what they become.** Before an exam is picked
  the rail's one card showed a single muted sentence, which reads as a failed
  load; it now carries greyed placeholder rows for the lines that will appear.
- **One icon per card title**, muted and `aria-hidden` — the text still carries
  the meaning. A form this long needs fixed points to scan by.

### What a member does **not** see

Ported from GarpAppv1, which is the authority on this — the branches are its
`IndividualDetailsCard`.

| Hidden for a member | Why |
|---|---|
| First name, last name, email | Already on their record, and registering does not change it. Showing them invites edits that look saved to the account but are not. The values stay in the form and still travel with the order — only the controls go |
| Nothing else | In particular **not** the phone or the promotional-SMS opt-in: GarpAppv1 shows both to everyone, and the phone is how exam-day changes reach a candidate |

**Location is not part of this.** It is gated on `!showAddresses`, for both
audiences — visible while no billing address card is on screen, hidden once one
is, because that card carries its own country and asking twice is two chances to
disagree. Gating it on login instead looks reasonable and is wrong: a member
with no billing country on file then has a required field they cannot see, and
Register stays disabled with nothing on screen to fix.

### The guest byline

Each programme has one line of copy shown under the title to guests only —
GarpAppv1's `publicByLine` in `programConfig.ts`. It is **static per programme**,
not computed: FRM's warns that returning Part II candidates must sign in
(their Part I record lives on an account); SCR's advertises a member rate. Port
the programme's own line; do not write a new one, and do not show it to members.

It is the upfront half of the same conversation the server has at submit via
`mustSignIn`, which is the point — signing in does not preserve what was typed.

### What a guest does **not** get

| Thing | Why |
|---|---|
| A back link | Every in-app parent is behind the session guard. Sending them to garp.org is *leaving*, not *back* — a back arrow must not promise that. Drop the adjacent divider with it |
| Any prefilled value | There is no contact record. `toFrmFormValues(null, …)` returns the empty set |
| Links to `/programs` or `/dashboard` | Both bounce to Login. The outcome screen offers garp.org and Sign In instead |
| The portal sidebar / alert bar | `_publicFormLayout` has its own chrome |

`_publicFormLayout`'s toolbar **must** stay `h-16` / `app:h-20` with a matching
spacer. This is load-bearing, not cosmetic: the forms size themselves with
`h-[calc(100vh-4rem)]` / `app:h-[calc(100vh-5rem)]`, so any other header height
pushes the sticky submit bar off-screen.

### Sign-in offers must be honest

Signing in is a full page navigation and **the form is not persisted**. Offer it
in the chrome, before anything is typed — not as a rescue halfway down. Where an
existing account is detected at submit (`mustSignIn`), the message must carry an
actual link and say plainly that they will start again.

Build the link with `getReturnPath(location)` and `LOGIN_PATH`. Do **not** copy
GarpAppv1's `handleSignIn`: it sends an absolute URL, which our own
`isSafeStartUrl` rejects, and its param casing differs from `AUTH_REDIRECT_PARAM`.

